# Game assets with new scene parts

Whenever a **new visible part of a game scene** is created (plant slot, card, UI chrome, pot, greenhouse frame, etc.), **generate a dedicated asset** for it in the same turn — do not leave placeholder-only geometry as the final look.

## Workflow

1. Identify what needs art (sprite, icon, background slice, etc.).
2. **If unsure** what asset, subject, framing, or size is needed → **ask the user** before generating.
3. Match **style of existing project assets** (palette, line weight, lighting, hand-drawn vs flat). Inspect files under `assets/` (or wherever art lives) and pass strong references via `reference_image_paths` when generating.
4. If no game assets exist yet, follow art direction in `dev_plan.md` and style of files under `ref/` (target mockups only). Then keep later assets consistent with shipped `assets/`.
5. Save into `assets/...`, wire into the scene (prefer Godot MCP `load_sprite` when applicable), and note non-obvious art choices in `dev_plan.md`.

## Style rules

- Same visual language as siblings already in the project — no one-off realism, neon UI, or unrelated genre looks.
- Transparent backgrounds for props/characters unless the piece is a full backdrop.
- Prefer one clear subject per asset; avoid cluttered mockups.

## Do not

- **Never use `hud.png` or anything under `ref/` as a runtime game texture.** Those are target mockups / art bible only. Always generate or place real assets in `assets/`.
- Ship a new gameplay-visible node without art when generation is possible.
- Guess subject or style when ambiguous — ask first.
- Ignore existing assets and invent a new look.
